173:The owner answered every row of the 2026-11-05 decision page, in the terminal. The rows that revise a recorded rule were answered one by one: Q5.1/Q5.4, Q6.4, Q2.8, Q9.15 with P3, Q7.11 and Q4.7. Q3.12, Q9.19, Q7.10, Q1.6, P2, P4, P9, P13, P16 and P17a/b were also answered directly. The remaining Core rows were accepted as recommended, and the remaining project rows were approved on the owner's condition that Claude and Codex agree and that each is the best long-term design (both confirmed; every row carries Codex agreement). The full rows (question, options, reason, recommendation, agreement, revision and notes) are in [core_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) and [other_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/other_rows.json); the concrete rules behind them are in [R4_RATIONALE.md](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md), and where R4 and a row differ, the row governs.
175:**The Core rows are a provisional approval (stage A2a).** They bind the design without freezing it. Stage A2b formalizes them in a proposed `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean` and proves the four design theorems; any row the formalization changes returns to the owner; stage A2i integrates the parts (2026-11-06); the owner freezes Core at A2c (row Q9.19 as revised). Until a rule is written in Lean, the answer below governs over any chapter text that differs.
179:- **OD45 and OD87** (rows Q5.1, Q5.4): capability arguments are written explicitly at every polymorphic call and checked against the callee; there is no capability inference and no inference solver. OD46 (type arguments from checked argument types) is unchanged.
180:- **TYPE-22@1's examples** (row Q6.4): a constant whose initializer provably fails is refused at compile time whether or not it is used (OD118); both examples, `unused-constant-never-runs` and `constant-traps-at-use`, become reject examples. When a constant is evaluated (at each use, never at start) is unchanged.
181:- **TRAIT-1@1** (row Q2.8): two impls with equal heads may coexist only when Copy/Destroy disjointness (OD28) makes their domains disjoint, as in `impl<T: Copy> Destroy for Vec<T, A>` beside `impl<T: Destroy> Destroy for Vec<T, A>`. No other coherence or orphan rule changes, and no disjunctive bound syntax is added.
182:- **The generated-chapter requirement** (rows Q9.15, P3): Lean is the specification, in one main normative file with doc comments and examples; no prose is generated from it. This confirms the [2026-11-04 decision](#decided-project-lean-is-the-spec). The "one main normative file" wording is revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): one authoritative specification library in cohesive modules, with `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean` as its public import.
183:- **Authority rules 13, 5 and 7** (row Q7.11): `Console` joins the reserved capability set, for operations on the `StdIn`, `StdOut` and `StdErr` grants; rule 5's operational list and rule 7's primitive table include it. `Device` stays for device grants.
184:- **The text-only slice of R4 F4.3** (row Q4.7) is replaced before it was recorded: one scoped form opens every view, `borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]`. Without `else` an invalid range traps, like indexing; with `else` it is handled; a proven invalid range without `else` is refused (OD118). Arrays need `a ≤ b ≤ len`; text also needs UTF-8 scalar boundaries at both byte offsets.
185:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields"></a>**Q6.2 and R4 F12, known values through structs** (owner-approved revision, 2026-11-05, made during A2b): known values propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring. The checker tracks an abstract value for each binding: unknown, a known scalar, or a constructor whose fields carry their own abstract values. Constructing a struct records each field's fact independently; an immutable binding keeps the fact; destructuring hands each field's fact, recursively, to the corresponding immutable binder; an unknown component stays unknown without discarding its known siblings; an ownership transfer keeps the facts for the receiving binding without permitting reuse of the consumed one. Q6.2's exclusions stay (mutable state, branches and paths, user-function calls, calls outside the table, elaborator temporaries). So `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 }; a + 100` is refused, in trusted and unreachable code too (OD118 as revised by Q6.4). Before this revision a field bound by destructuring was not known and that program was accepted and trapped.
186:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q9-1-reads"></a>**Q9.1, read forms** (owner-confirmed, 2026-11-05): `copy x` is allowed only when `x` has a Copy type and `move x` only when it has a non-Copy (linear) type. Each read has one form; `move` of a Copy value and `copy` of a linear value are both refused.
192:- **Q1.1** What is an allocator brand? — Separate erased `Brand` kind.
193:- **Q1.2** How are fresh brands introduced, including loop resets? — Generative binders; existential loop state.
194:- **Q1.3** How are packages formed and opened? — Explicit pack; lexical unpack; repackage escapes.
195:- **Q1.4** How are brands compared and supplied? — Nominal brand equality; explicit brand arguments.
196:- **Q1.5** How are allocator dependencies tracked across calls? — Structural dependencies; checked interface summaries.
197:- **Q1.6** Who owns a child allocator’s backing? — Child owns backing; parent brand exposed in type. *Revises or adds: Clarify/extend allocator interface.*
198:- **Q1.7** Where does release information live? — Stable release state and raw context; no safe references.
199:- **Q1.8** What if creating allocator state fails? — Fallible allocator construction conserving surviving inputs.
200:- **Q1.9** What do allocation failures contain? — `AllocFailure<T>` returns rejected `T`; operation-specific recovery.
201:- **Q1.10** Are capacity and addresses observable? — Observable capacity; explicit raw-address exposure.
202:- **Q1.11** What does “unchanged” require of allocator state? — Availability predicate plus four laws: progress, unchanged state on failure, release never reduces availability, a concrete success witness.
203:- **Q1.12** What rolls back around callbacks? — No rollback of callback effects; every element accounted for in a typed result.
204:- **Q1.13** Can resetting the system allocator return `Allocator<Sys>` again? — Fresh brand after any supported system reset.
205:- **Q2.1** Are storage release, destruction and resource cleanup separate? — Separate storage release, destruction and resource cleanup.
206:- **Q2.2** Which containers implement `Destroy`? — Exactly sixteen named Destroy impls: Array (T: Destroy); Heap, HeapArray and Vec (two each: T: Copy; T: Destroy); String (unconditional); Option (T: Destroy); Result (three: T: Destroy/E: Copy, T: Destroy/E: Destroy, T: Copy/E: Destroy); OrderedMap (four: each of K and V Copy or Destroy). HashMap excluded. <a id="q2-2-revision"></a>Revised 2026-11-06 by [information flow](#decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06): `Secret<T>` is `Copy` only for scalar `T`; secret arrays and heap secrets are linear and their destruction wipes them.
207:- **Q2.3** In what order are contents destroyed? — Index order 0..len−1, then the backing; OrderedMap in ascending key order, key then value, then the nodes.
208:- **Q2.4** How does a container of non-`Destroy` resources release its storage? — `drain(v, ctx, f)` with `f: fn(&mut C, T) with(K) -> Step<T, E>`; the only way to empty a container of non-Destroy elements.
209:- **Q2.5** What happens when drain processing fails? — On Stop, `Drained { at, survivor, rest, err }`; `map_into` returns `Stopped { done, survivor, rest, err }`.
210:- **Q2.6** Must `destroy` terminate and avoid traps? — Ordinary partial correctness; prove stronger guarantees separately.
211:- **Q2.7** Does user destruction have special execution semantics? — Ordinary consuming checked `destroy`; at most `Alloc`.
212:- **Q2.8** How is 'Copy or Destroy' written for container impls? — (a) separate impls for every admitted combination of Copy/Destroy payload bounds, no disjunctive-bound syntax: two each for Heap, HeapArray and Vec, three for Result, four for OrderedMap; coherence may use Copy/Destroy disjointness. *Revises or adds: TRAIT-1@1 (under OD6): equal generic impl heads allowed only when Copy/Destroy disjointness makes their domains disjoint; all other coherence and orphan rules unchanged. Option (b) would also add new bound syntax.*
213:- **Q3.1** What does opening a heap array produce? — Second-class shared/mutable array views; no element references.
214:- **Q3.2** How are views passed and lent? — Downward parameters; mutable lending freezes origin.
215:- **Q3.3** What is a view’s root identity? — Original owner identity plus projection path.
216:- **Q3.4** Which view accesses conflict? — Existing whole-root conflict rules.
217:- **Q3.5** What is the index type? — `u64` lengths/indices across profiles; checked target limits.
218:- **Q3.6** How are nested linear elements traversed without substitutes? — `drain` / `map_into` for consuming traversal; `swap_at(v, i, x) -> T` for in-place replacement.
219:- **Q3.7** What is the raw-memory model and validity taxonomy? — Blocks with provenance; pointer = (prov: Option<BlockId>, addr: u64); integers carry no provenance; every access needs explicit validity premises.
220:- **Q3.8** How do zero-sized elements behave? — Zero-sized slots own their obligations; they occupy no bytes and may share addresses.
221:- **Q3.9** What do pointer equality and integer round trips mean? — `expose` records the block; `from_exposed` picks in-bounds, then one-past, else no provenance.
222:- **Q3.10** What happens when address space runs out? — A collision means overlapping occupied bytes; address exhaustion is part of Avail.
223:- **Q3.11** How are typed values related to raw bytes? — Structured values and raw store joined by `Rep`.
224:- **Q3.12** How are extern pointer parameters marked, and how does C keep memory past the call? — (a) `kept` parameter marker; `Kept<H>` receipt ended only by the paired extern. *Revises or adds: New design + spelling: owner decision.*
225:- **Q4.1** What is a literal’s type? — Copy `StaticStr`; explicit fallible dynamic conversion.
226:- **Q4.2** Which encoding and NUL policy apply? — UTF-8 including embedded NUL.
227:- **Q4.3** What do length and comparison mean? — Byte length; content equality; lexicographic byte order.
228:- **Q4.4** Are literal addresses observable and literals mergeable? — Content semantics; pooling allowed; no safe address identity.
229:- **Q4.5** What is the FFI representation? — Explicit pointer/byte-length FFI adapter.
230:- **Q4.6** What are dynamic string views? — Byte offsets; `byte_at` traps out of range; subviews only through the scoped borrow form of Q4.7 (`borrow t from a until b as s { … } [else { … }]`). *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): byte_at needs evidence of an in-range offset.*
231:- **Q4.7** How are scoped views and range subviews introduced? — (a) borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]; no else = trap (like indexing); proven invalid range without else refused (OD118). *Revises or adds: Replaces R4 F4.3 text-only mandatory-else form; extends Q3.1/Q3.2 with subview formation; preserves OD36/OD118.* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the form without `else` needs evidence of a valid range; a provably out-of-range view index is covered by the same rule.*
232:- **Q5.1** Should capability arguments ever be inferred? — Explicit capability arguments on every polymorphic call. *Revises or adds: OD45, OD87.*
233:- **Q5.2** May explicit arguments contain the caller’s capability variables? — Explicit symbolic capability expressions and union.
234:- **Q5.3** What is capability-set equality and normal form? — Canonical semantic equality; refuse repeated names.
235:- **Q5.4** Is a capability inference solver still normative? — Explicit substitution checking; no inference solver. *Revises or adds: OD45, OD87.*
236:- **Q6.1** What determines mandatory static refusal? — Fixed Lean-defined local refusal rules. *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): static refusal widens from proven violations to unproven safety.*
237:- **Q6.2** Is local constant propagation mandatory? — Fixed table of statically known values: literals, immutable lets, module constants, checked ops, six named wrapping/saturating ops, lossless `as`. *Revised 2026-11-05: facts propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring ([revision](#decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields)).* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): known values remain facts; refusal widens to unproven safety.*
238:- **Q6.3** Are violations rechecked after generic substitution? — Parametric checking plus substitution-time rechecking.
239:- **Q6.4** Does unreachable code receive static refusal checks? — Check all written code, including generic bodies. *Revises or adds: REVISES TYPE-22@1 examples.*
240:- **Q6.5** How do short-circuit operators interact with refusal? — Check both short-circuit operands; execute conditionally.
241:- **Q6.6** What happens when constant evaluation fails? — Separate invalid and unknown; no evaluator-resource failure.
242:- **Q6.7** What distinguishes traps, unsafe stuckness and exhaustion? — Separate traps, unsafe stuckness, OOM and fuel exhaustion.
243:- **Q7.1** How are target grant schemas and multiplicities specified? — One target, linux-x86_64-hosted (Linux ≥ 5.6), with a fixed grant table; main asks for grants as parameters.
244:- **Q7.2** How do narrowing, acquisition and splitting relate? — Narrowing consumes and intersects rights; children are acquired from a grant that stays.
245:- **Q7.3** What are close and close-error contracts? — close always consumes; failure is a Copy error holding no handle; listen failure returns its grant.
246:- **Q7.4** How does process inheritance work? — Deferred: this target offers no process spawning.
247:- **Q7.5** Which environment does a program observe? — Scoped immutable launch environment snapshot.
248:- **Q7.6** What does a clock grant specify? — Explicit clock identity, kind, resolution and guarantees.
249:- **Q7.7** What are network scopes? — Connect and listen grants with numeric scopes; a separate Resolver grant with explicit server, transport, suffixes and record types.
250:- **Q7.8** Is grant policy enforced statically or dynamically? — Static authority checks plus atomic dynamic scope checks.
251:- **Q7.9** How are blocking, terminal actions and delivery observed? — Split request/response events; logical terminal observations.
252:- **Q7.10** Do you approve the linux-x86_64-hosted grant table? — Approve R4 F6's complete grant table below as part of this row: every launch configuration, operation signature, permission, narrowing rule and ending contract. Standard-stream operations require Console (Q7.11), not Device; where the R4 F6 table says with(Device) for StdIn/StdOut/StdErr, read with(Console). A missing configured grant refuses startup with exit 125; a trap exits 134. *Revises or adds: New target contract: owner decision.* *Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the stack target contract (adequate stack excludes exhaustion, launch refusal 125 when the reservation is smaller than the bound; then a defined outcome for inadequate stack) is a future target-contract obligation, not a current guarantee.*
253:- **Q7.11** Which permission do the standard streams use? — Add a Console capability for StdIn/StdOut/StdErr operations; keep Device for device grants. *Revises or adds: Authority rule 13 (adds Console); rules 5 and 7 lists include it.*
254:- **Q8.1** What is the first proof endpoint? — Proved source→BIR; qualified native; conditional image claim.
255:- **Q8.2** What exact source subset is admitted? — A1 minimal `u8`/linear-struct component subset. <a id="q8-2-revision"></a>Confirmed 2026-11-06 by [vertical slices](#decided-project-vertical-slices-2026-11-06): this is slice 1; its defined trap is an explicit `abort()`, with the refused `200 + 56` as its known-violation control; integers are complete at slice 2.
256:- **Q8.3** What establishes frontend correspondence? — Independent byte-to-Core correspondence judgments.
257:- **Q8.4** Does the first artifact qualify as a complete executable? — Component plus bound launcher first.
258:- **Q8.5** What launcher ABI and observations are fixed? — Pinned launcher ABI, inputs, startup and trap mapping. *Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the stack target contract (adequate stack excludes exhaustion, launch refusal 125 when the reservation is smaller than the bound; then a defined outcome for inadequate stack) is a future target-contract obligation, not a current guarantee.*
259:- **Q8.6** What artifacts does the claim bind? — Bind complete retained dependency and image closure.
260:- **Q8.7** What prevents vacuous correctness? — Coverage, realizable witnesses and rejection controls; a spec where every allocation fails fails qualification.
261:- **Q9.1** What is Core’s expression and binding form? — ANF; fresh identities; explicit moves/copies; separate places. *Confirmed 2026-11-05: `copy` only for Copy types, `move` only for non-Copy types ([read forms](#decided-2026-11-05-q9-1-reads)).*
262:- **Q9.2** What types and declaration identities must Core represent? — Kinded nominal inventory; ordinary owning container definitions.
263:- **Q9.3** What is the single loop form? — Core `while L { C } do { B }`: C is a condition region re-run every iteration; the counted loop advances its counter before the body.
264:- **Q9.4** How are data, matching and exits represented? — Flat data/matches; structured exits; checked stores.
265:- **Q9.5** Are heap opening and borrowing separate user mechanisms? — One surface borrow; distinct typed Core cases.
266:- **Q9.6** Which operations are primitives rather than ordinary definitions? — Irreducible primitives; checked destructor bodies.
267:- **Q9.7** How are primitive calls specified? — One exhaustive typed primitive-contract inventory.
268:- **Q9.8** Does `defer` survive in Core? — Nested deferred scopes; late reads; reverse cleanup.
269:- **Q9.9** What is evaluator state and execution style? — Total clock-indexed evaluator; explicit instrumented state.
270:- **Q9.10** How are ownership instrumentation and erasure organized? — Ghost ownership instrumentation with proved erasure.
271:- **Q9.11** What do contracts mean and when are they checked? — Erased logical contracts; ensures sees result and surviving parameters after cleanup; no snapshot construct. *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a claim used for safety admission needs closed evidence.*
272:- **Q9.12** What evidence crosses declaration/module interfaces? — Checked semantic interfaces with reviewable declarations.
273:- **Q9.13** Are judgments independent of the checker and elaborator? — Independent judgments; checker/elaborator correspondence.
274:- **Q9.14** What must pass before Core freezes? — Provisional approval, then four kernel-checked theorems against a proposed Spec.lean, then freeze. Revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): the integration stage A2i (interaction proofs, semantic contract, modular layout, owner view, outside-reader pass) also passes before the freeze.
275:- **Q9.15** Where do normative definitions and documentation live? — One main normative Lean file with doc comments. *Revises or adds: Generated-chapter requirement (philosophy rule 6 / earlier roadmap commitment): replaced by one normative Lean file with doc comments and examples, a browsable rendering of that Lean, and coverage-gated retirement of prose chapters; your decision of 2026-11-04.*
276:- **Q9.16** What Lean dependencies may the specification use? — Core Lean; supporting Batteries; owner exceptions for Mathlib.
277:- **Q9.17** May spec proofs use `native_decide`? — Kernel-only authoritative spec/A2 proofs.
278:- **Q9.18** How is the old compiler retained? — Frozen isolated `legacy/`; no normative fallback.
279:- **Q9.19** How do approval, formalization and Core freeze relate? — A2a → A2b → A2c; Spec.lean headed PROPOSED until freeze; R-0560 depends on the freeze. *Revises or adds: Process: owner decision.* Revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): integration (A2i) comes between A2b and the A2c freeze, and the modular layout (R-0560) precedes the freeze.
285:- **P3** Lean is the spec: confirm the amendment — Lean is the specification: confirmed together with Q9.15 (revision below).
290:- **P8** Server services under scripts/ — Keep only landing services: train, review, hook, landing bot. The status page, work queue, fuzz farm, perf tracking and nightly campaign all stop and are recorded as archived in RETENTION.tsv; each returns only with a roadmap task.
297:- **P15** New roadmap: retiring obligations — Accept the new single-file ROADMAP.md (wip/new-roadmap, reconciled: prepare → rehearse → move, assurance wording per Q8.1, Phase E per P18, decides nothing itself): 79 tasks kept as rows, 345 folded into area tasks with their obligations, 109 deferred with triggers, 7 retired, 15 done.
301:- **P18** Phase E: contracts and termination within OD115–OD117 — Phase E: no source old, quantifier, implication or snapshot construct (OD117); entry/exit and frame properties are Lean theorems (a source frame clause would be a new decision for you); spec functions have a body and are total (OD115); ordinary functions have no total category (OD116) and get per-function termination theorems where wanted. R-0524 becomes 'Frame and entry/exit theorems in Lean, no old'; R-0526 stays 'checked totality of spec functions'.
306:- **P23** Precedence between decisions, rows and drafts — Recorded decisions govern. An accepted row supersedes a recorded rule only through an explicitly identified, owner-approved revision (Q5.1/Q5.4 for OD45/OD87, Q6.4 for TYPE-22, Q2.8 for TRAIT-1, Q9.15/P3 for generated chapters). R4's replacements govern conflicting draft recommendations. Before your acceptance is recorded, every passage R4 F18 lists is reconciled (capability inference, generated chapters, roadmap authority, assurance wording, restart order and retention, Phase E, freeze dependencies, superseded Core recommendations), and historical conflicting text is marked superseded.
