docs/decisions.md:173:The owner answered every row of the 2026-11-05 decision page, in the terminal. The rows that revise a recorded rule were answered one by one: Q5.1/Q5.4, Q6.4, Q2.8, Q9.15 with P3, Q7.11 and Q4.7. Q3.12, Q9.19, Q7.10, Q1.6, P2, P4, P9, P13, P16 and P17a/b were also answered directly. The remaining Core rows were accepted as recommended, and the remaining project rows were approved on the owner's condition that Claude and Codex agree and that each is the best long-term design (both confirmed; every row carries Codex agreement). The full rows (question, options, reason, recommendation, agreement, revision and notes) are in [core_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) and [other_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/other_rows.json); the concrete rules behind them are in [R4_RATIONALE.md](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md), and where R4 and a row differ, the row governs.
docs/decisions.md:179:- **OD45 and OD87** (rows Q5.1, Q5.4): capability arguments are written explicitly at every polymorphic call and checked against the callee; there is no capability inference and no inference solver. OD46 (type arguments from checked argument types) is unchanged.
docs/decisions.md:180:- **TYPE-22@1's examples** (row Q6.4): a constant whose initializer provably fails is refused at compile time whether or not it is used (OD118); both examples, `unused-constant-never-runs` and `constant-traps-at-use`, become reject examples. When a constant is evaluated (at each use, never at start) is unchanged.
docs/decisions.md:184:- **The text-only slice of R4 F4.3** (row Q4.7) is replaced before it was recorded: one scoped form opens every view, `borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]`. Without `else` an invalid range traps, like indexing; with `else` it is handled; a proven invalid range without `else` is refused (OD118). Arrays need `a ≤ b ≤ len`; text also needs UTF-8 scalar boundaries at both byte offsets.
docs/decisions.md:185:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields"></a>**Q6.2 and R4 F12, known values through structs** (owner-approved revision, 2026-11-05, made during A2b): known values propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring. The checker tracks an abstract value for each binding: unknown, a known scalar, or a constructor whose fields carry their own abstract values. Constructing a struct records each field's fact independently; an immutable binding keeps the fact; destructuring hands each field's fact, recursively, to the corresponding immutable binder; an unknown component stays unknown without discarding its known siblings; an ownership transfer keeps the facts for the receiving binding without permitting reuse of the consumed one. Q6.2's exclusions stay (mutable state, branches and paths, user-function calls, calls outside the table, elaborator temporaries). So `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 }; a + 100` is refused, in trusted and unreachable code too (OD118 as revised by Q6.4). Before this revision a field bound by destructuring was not known and that program was accepted and trapped.
docs/decisions.md:203:- **Q1.12** What rolls back around callbacks? — No rollback of callback effects; every element accounted for in a typed result.
docs/decisions.md:221:- **Q3.9** What do pointer equality and integer round trips mean? — `expose` records the block; `from_exposed` picks in-bounds, then one-past, else no provenance.
docs/decisions.md:225:- **Q4.1** What is a literal’s type? — Copy `StaticStr`; explicit fallible dynamic conversion.
docs/decisions.md:226:- **Q4.2** Which encoding and NUL policy apply? — UTF-8 including embedded NUL.
docs/decisions.md:227:- **Q4.3** What do length and comparison mean? — Byte length; content equality; lexicographic byte order.
docs/decisions.md:228:- **Q4.4** Are literal addresses observable and literals mergeable? — Content semantics; pooling allowed; no safe address identity.
docs/decisions.md:229:- **Q4.5** What is the FFI representation? — Explicit pointer/byte-length FFI adapter.
docs/decisions.md:230:- **Q4.6** What are dynamic string views? — Byte offsets; `byte_at` traps out of range; subviews only through the scoped borrow form of Q4.7 (`borrow t from a until b as s { … } [else { … }]`). *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): byte_at needs evidence of an in-range offset.*
docs/decisions.md:231:- **Q4.7** How are scoped views and range subviews introduced? — (a) borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]; no else = trap (like indexing); proven invalid range without else refused (OD118). *Revises or adds: Replaces R4 F4.3 text-only mandatory-else form; extends Q3.1/Q3.2 with subview formation; preserves OD36/OD118.* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the form without `else` needs evidence of a valid range; a provably out-of-range view index is covered by the same rule.*
docs/decisions.md:232:- **Q5.1** Should capability arguments ever be inferred? — Explicit capability arguments on every polymorphic call. *Revises or adds: OD45, OD87.*
docs/decisions.md:233:- **Q5.2** May explicit arguments contain the caller’s capability variables? — Explicit symbolic capability expressions and union.
docs/decisions.md:234:- **Q5.3** What is capability-set equality and normal form? — Canonical semantic equality; refuse repeated names.
docs/decisions.md:235:- **Q5.4** Is a capability inference solver still normative? — Explicit substitution checking; no inference solver. *Revises or adds: OD45, OD87.*
docs/decisions.md:236:- **Q6.1** What determines mandatory static refusal? — Fixed Lean-defined local refusal rules. *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): static refusal widens from proven violations to unproven safety.*
docs/decisions.md:237:- **Q6.2** Is local constant propagation mandatory? — Fixed table of statically known values: literals, immutable lets, module constants, checked ops, six named wrapping/saturating ops, lossless `as`. *Revised 2026-11-05: facts propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring ([revision](#decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields)).* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): known values remain facts; refusal widens to unproven safety.*
docs/decisions.md:238:- **Q6.3** Are violations rechecked after generic substitution? — Parametric checking plus substitution-time rechecking.
docs/decisions.md:239:- **Q6.4** Does unreachable code receive static refusal checks? — Check all written code, including generic bodies. *Revises or adds: REVISES TYPE-22@1 examples.*
docs/decisions.md:240:- **Q6.5** How do short-circuit operators interact with refusal? — Check both short-circuit operands; execute conditionally.
docs/decisions.md:241:- **Q6.6** What happens when constant evaluation fails? — Separate invalid and unknown; no evaluator-resource failure.
docs/decisions.md:242:- **Q6.7** What distinguishes traps, unsafe stuckness and exhaustion? — Separate traps, unsafe stuckness, OOM and fuel exhaustion.
docs/decisions.md:291:- **P9** Website during the transition — The website goes down at the move and is redone from scratch with the new compiler; no frozen playground is served. Unpublishing the live site is an outward action whose moment the owner confirms.
docs/decisions.md:306:- **P23** Precedence between decisions, rows and drafts — Recorded decisions govern. An accepted row supersedes a recorded rule only through an explicitly identified, owner-approved revision (Q5.1/Q5.4 for OD45/OD87, Q6.4 for TYPE-22, Q2.8 for TRAIT-1, Q9.15/P3 for generated chapters). R4's replacements govern conflicting draft recommendations. Before your acceptance is recorded, every passage R4 F18 lists is reconciled (capability inference, generated chapters, roadmap authority, assurance wording, restart order and retention, Phase E, freeze dependencies, superseded Core recommendations), and historical conflicting text is marked superseded.
