400:`deferred_call` is a parsed postfix call whose final suffix is `call_args` (a method call counts); its result must be Unit. It shares the section-5 postfix parser and rejects a binary expression, constructor or block. That syntactic restriction is the OPEN-19 recommendation. `statement_block` means a braced statement-mode block under the OPEN-16 algorithm; `arm_block` uses the surrounding match mode; `arm_expr` means section-5 expr without a block-root alternative. `value_contents` is precisely the sequence/tail selection described in that algorithm, whose EBNF expansion remains OPEN-16. These are explicit parameterized parsing obligations, not unexplained extra terminals.
404:## 5. Expressions, paths, constructors and contracts
430:              | IDENT, path_tail, [constructor_body]
440:constructor_body = "{", [field_value, field_value_tail], "}" ;
448:All expression auxiliaries inherit the slices of their operators/atoms from section 2. `path_tail` and arguments arrive at 11 for generics, 14 for associated/trait calls, 17 for module paths, and 10 for `Enum::Variant`; simple calls at 5. The proposed path production parses a common neutral path and defers declaration classification; it does not approve arguments on arbitrary intermediate module segments. Those restrictions and explicit generic enum constructors are **OPEN-14/20**. The syntax distinguishes `f::<T>(x)` from `x < y`, with a single-token choice after `::`: `<` starts arguments, IDENT continues the path. Actual calls to a function value become legal only at slice 20.
450:`head_expr` is the same precedence hierarchy with a separate **head primary**: at enclosing `(`/`[` depth zero it has no constructor-body suffix. Entering grouping, call parentheses, array brackets or index brackets restores ordinary expr for the contents. Thus `if LIMIT {…}` ends the head, while `if test(P { x: 1 }) {…}` permits a literal inside parentheses. `as` also terminates the source of a range borrow, so that source must use the place grammar below rather than unrestricted expr. [OD16](../docs/decisions.md#od16), [OD34](../docs/decisions.md#od34) fix the head rule, not a casing convention.
467:| constructors `S { f: e }`, `E::V { f: e }` | enum separator [OD77](../docs/decisions.md#od77), argument-based enum inference, otherwise explicit [OD129](../docs/decisions.md#od129); struct literal shell slice 1; explicit expression fields recommended under OPEN-20; empty struct literal already admitted by OD65 and approved slice 1 |
524:Raw types are `*const T` / `*mut T` (slice 24). Dereference/store use unary `*` and place assignment; `as` is not a blanket pointer/integer conversion mechanism: reference-to-raw conversion is gated by [OD98](../docs/decisions.md#od98). Reference-to-reference casts are refused ([OD103](../docs/decisions.md#od103)); pointer/integer round trips use Q3.9’s `expose` / `from_exposed`, not an invented `as` conversion. Integers carry no pointer provenance; every raw access requires typed validity/provenance/aliasing premises (Core Q3.7–Q3.11). Reference-derived pointers stay confined to the borrow and can never reach a kept parameter; kept retention transfers an owned allocation H by value and returns a linear Kept<H> receipt ([OD64](../docs/decisions.md#od64), Core Q3.12). A write derived from shared &T remains forbidden ([OD112](../docs/decisions.md#od112)).
537:| Downward view parameter types | 15/16/21 | shared/mutable views, no element refs; Q3/Q4.7 | **OPEN-21**: `View<T>` / `ViewMut<T>` as recognized second-class type constructors; alternative `&[T]` / `&mut [T]` (would require an unsized type form) |
538:| Function values with context | 20 | function pointer plus explicit context, exact capability set | **OPEN-22**: reuse named functions plus explicit context parameters; alternative explicit pair constructor; no closure syntax chosen |
604:| bare `*T` | [OD74](../docs/decisions.md#od74) | choose *const or *mut |
629:3. **OPEN-3 — Full keyword table and contextual tokens (0–25).** Recommend the complete reserved list in section 2, retain self/repr/packed/align contextual, reserve spec/by/kept/Self, and propose `Entropy` for the new entropy capability subject to owner approval. Alternative: keep spec/by/kept contextual with a fully factored token model, while retaining the existing special Self type meaning. Reason: preserve explicit existing reservations and contextual self under OD90 while making contextual FIRST sets honest. Also decide whether obsolete in/newtype/union/total stay reserved for diagnostics; no current row settles that complete set.
636:10. **OPEN-10 — Alias/constant spelling (9/17).** Recommend `type N = T;` and `const N: T = e;`, no generic aliases unless separately approved. Alternative: `alias N = T;` and inferred constants. Reason: retain historical spellings while acknowledging that semantic approval alone does not settle each delimiter.
646:20. **OPEN-20 — Constructors/projections/array expression details (1/9/10/15/17).** Recommend declaration-neutral paths, explicit constructor field values (no expression puns); retain decided pattern punning and empty struct literals, nonempty array list literals (TYPE-7) and repeat arrays (zero-count policy still for approval), no tuple projection, place-based field/index access, calls to values only at 20; explicitly factor trailing commas. Alternative: retain expression puns. Reason: explicit values distinguish field labels from moved locals; close historical conveniences without extending removed newtypes or call-result projections. Choose exact generic enum construction placement and grouped/dereference place grammar.
647:21. **OPEN-21 — API/view spellings and branded signatures (7/15/16/19/21–27).** Recommend View/ViewMut second-class parameter constructors and ordinary calls for the decided ct_select/ct_eq/text/container APIs; use exact explicit branded type arguments; floating conversions use `name::<Target>(x)`. Alternative: &[]-style view types and qualified associated APIs. Reason: feature names in Q3/Q4 and the information-flow decision do not decide every arity, type position, qualifier or conversion target syntax. Submit the exhaustive concrete signature table before encoding it; no fabricated complete signatures here.
648:22. **OPEN-22 — Brand/package/function-contract surface (19/20).** Recommend the explicit brand/exists/pack/unpack proposals in section 7 and named binder contracts on fn types, reusing ordinary explicit context parameters for function values. Alternative: scoped library eliminators plus named contract aliases. Reason: Core Q1 approves these mechanisms, while R-0601 explicitly still owns a binding design; surface syntax cannot be recovered from ANF constructors. Approve each binder, packaging expression, escape and loop-carried existential rule separately.
