_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:479:`contract_expr` uses the expression operator grammar over a restricted, pure, non-consuming specification judgment. It is **not** a second permissive executable language. Ordinary runtime calls, allocation, mutation, linear consumption and capabilities do not become contract operations. Calls are to total `spec fn` and explicitly permitted machine operations. Mathematical integer arithmetic is the default; named machine operations retain machine meaning ([OD12](../docs/decisions.md#od12), [OD145](../docs/decisions.md#od145), [OD115](../docs/decisions.md#od115), [OD116](../docs/decisions.md#od116), [CON-3](../legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md#CON-3)). The exact syntactic restriction and allowed specification atom/type vocabulary is **OPEN-13**; the parser can share syntax, while a separate judgment rejects forbidden forms.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:481:`requires` sees parameters at entry and permitted declarations; `ensures` sees result and surviving parameters after cleanup, never body locals or an implicit entry snapshot; loop clauses/assertions see their lexical scope including eligible ghost locals. The November 2 [audit](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit) refines [OD143](../docs/decisions.md#od143). No `old`, quantifier, implication, equivalence or snapshot source form ([OD117](../docs/decisions.md#od117)); such properties are Lean theorems. `#[proof(T)]` means a typed theorem path T, not a type argument or string.
