516:Slice 13 introduces ordinary reference borrows; 15 array range views; 16 text ranges; 19 heap-cell opening; 21 heap-array opening/views. The spellings `borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view {…} [else {…}]` are decided by [Core Q4.7/Q9.5](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-core). There is no `in R` ([OD57](../docs/decisions.md#od57)), and no other heap opening form ([OD36](../docs/decisions.md#od36)). No-range else is not proposed; Q4.7's precise optional-clause combination is **OPEN-19**.
520:The source is a place rooted in a binding; exact allowed source projections/grouping is OPEN-19/20. Opening a heap array produces a second-class shared/mutable view, **never an element reference**. Views pass downward only, retain owner identity, obey whole-root conflicts, and use u64 lengths/indices (Core Q3.1–Q3.6). Explicit type names for views are OPEN-21. A no-else range requires evidence of validity; else is the explicit recovery path, not a permission to trap implicitly.
524:Raw types are `*const T` / `*mut T` (slice 24). Dereference/store use unary `*` and place assignment; `as` is not a blanket pointer/integer conversion mechanism: reference-to-raw conversion is gated by [OD98](../docs/decisions.md#od98). Reference-to-reference casts are refused ([OD103](../docs/decisions.md#od103)); pointer/integer round trips use Q3.9’s `expose` / `from_exposed`, not an invented `as` conversion. Integers carry no pointer provenance; every raw access requires typed validity/provenance/aliasing premises (Core Q3.7–Q3.11). Reference-derived pointers stay confined to the borrow and can never reach a kept parameter; kept retention transfers an owned allocation H by value and returns a linear Kept<H> receipt ([OD64](../docs/decisions.md#od64), Core Q3.12). A write derived from shared &T remains forbidden ([OD112](../docs/decisions.md#od112)).
526:Slice 25 declares C bindings as `extern fn`, optionally `trusted extern fn`, explicit `with(...)`, mandatory semicolon and no body. `#[link_name = "symbol"]` and `#[export_name = "symbol"]` payload forms are recommendations (OPEN-15), not an inferred `extern "C" {…}` block. Direct calls only: foreign function values and `extern "C" fn` types are not admitted ([OD4](../docs/decisions.md#od4)). FFI-safe types exclude enums ([OD110](../docs/decisions.md#od110)); C structs cross by value at every size ([OD122](../docs/decisions.md#od122)). `kept buf: H`, no marker meaning call-only, and the paired receipt discipline are already decided by the [full Q3.12 row](../docs/archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json). The submit contract names a paired extern taking exactly Kept<H> and returning H; borrow-derived pointers are forbidden there. A kept raw pointer is not a substitute for the owned allocation. OPEN-23 concerns remaining raw API qualification and transcription, not these settled choices. Semantic validity obligations must not be replaced by inventing an `unsafe {…}` source block.
